<span id="S2.T1.7.7.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.7.7.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Forward computation</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S2.T1.7.7.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:11.4pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.7.7.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S2.T1.7.7.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:105.3pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.7.7.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.7.7.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Input gradient computation</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.7.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.7.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb"></td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"></td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S2.T1.7.8.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:11.4pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.7.8.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S2.T1.7.8.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:105.3pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.7.8.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.7.8.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Parameter gradient computation</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Parallelism (DP)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sergeev and Del Balso, 2018</a>)</cite> splits the input training data into smaller mini-batches. Each device maintains a copy of parameters and takes its assigned mini-batches as input. When applying sharding techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajbhandari et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, the redundant memory overhead can be reduced.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tensor Parallelism (TP)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shoeybi et al., 2019</a>)</cite> partitions a layer into smaller layer shards across multiple GPUs. TP alleviates the GPU memory pressure but compensates for heavy collective communication (e.g., <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">all-reduce</span>). Hence, TP is usually adopted within a single node to avoid cross-node communication <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Narayanan et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pipeline Parallelism (PP)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2019</a>)</cite> introduces point to point (P2P) communication to efficiently support cross node communication. As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1. Distributed Training Parallelisms ‣ 2. Background ‣ AdaPtis: Reducing Pipeline Bubbles with Adaptive Pipeline Parallelism on Heterogeneous Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, PP consists of three phases, including model partition, model placement, and workload scheduling. First, the model is divided into sequential pipeline stages, each comprising a set of consecutive layers. Second, these stages are assigned to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> groups of devices. Intermediate results are transmitted via P2P communication between devices hosting adjacent stages. Third, the input mini-batch is further subdivided into micro-batches, which are processed in a pipelined manner across all stages to improve device utilization. We next introduce these three phases of PP.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.23722v1/pipeline_plan_decoupling.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/394;" width="476" height="394" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustrations of Model Partition, Model Placement, and Workload Scheduling in Pipeline Parallelism.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Model Partition</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the beginning of pipeline parallelism, the model is partitioned into multiple stages. A common strategy is to evenly allocate transformer layers across stages, with the input layer assigned to the first stage and the output layer to the final stage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shoeybi et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajbhandari et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harlap et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Narayanan et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. With the growing heterogeneity of model architectures, this model partition scheme inevitably leads to an increasing size of bubbles. To reduce bubbles, recent approaches adjust the number of layers per stage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Um et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. These methods formulate the model partition task as an Integer Linear Programming (ILP) problem, which is solved through dynamic programming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Um et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or ILP solvers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gurobi Optimization, LLC, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Moura and Bjørner, 2008</a>)</cite>. While these approaches mitigate imbalance to some extent, methods such as Mist <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> still exhibit substantial bubbles when training heterogeneous models like Nemotron-H <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blakeman et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ AdaPtis: Reducing Pipeline Bubbles with Adaptive Pipeline Parallelism on Heterogeneous Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Model Placement</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model placement refers to mapping the pipeline stages onto physical devices, which determines the inter-device data dependencies. A widely adopted strategy is to sequentially map the stages to devices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harlap et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Um et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This approach assumes that the number of stages equals the pipeline parallelism size (<math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=P</annotation></semantics></math>), with each stage assigned to a unique device. To reduce bubbles at the beginning and end of each training step, I-1F1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Narayanan et al., 2021</a>)</cite> introduces the <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">virtual pipeline stages</span>, which split pipeline stages into smaller ones. Hanayo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> builds upon this idea but applies a wave-like stage-to-device mapping policy. Other approaches, such as Chimera <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Hoefler, 2021</a>)</cite> and DualPipe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, duplicate model parameters to form multiple pipelines, allowing for the concurrent execution of multiple micro-batches across model replicas. However, the same benefits can be achieved through <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">virtual pipeline stages</span> without the memory redundancy introduced by parameter duplication <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. All these methods rely on manually defined model placement policies (e.g., manually setting the number of <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">virtual pipeline stages</span> in I-1F1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Narayanan et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, pipelines in Chimera <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Hoefler, 2021</a>)</cite>, or waves in Hanayo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>), which limits their adaptivity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Workload Scheduling</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the inter-device data dependencies defined by model placement, the workloads, consisting of the forward pass (<math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>) and the backward pass of each micro-batch, are scheduled according to a predefined execution order. When the backward splitting strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> is applied, the backward pass is further divided into input gradient computation (<math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>) and parameter gradient computation (<math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The execution order can be manually designed, as in GPipe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and S-1F1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shoeybi et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, or automatically generated, as in ZB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> and Tessel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Manually designed orders are fixed; therefore, they can not adapt to configuration changes. Automatic scheduling methods model the problem as a Job Shop Scheduling Problem (JSSP), which is NP hard, and employ ILP Solvers to obtain workload scheduling policies. However, the large search space makes finding the optimal workload scheduling infeasible in practice. For example, Tessel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> restricts the search space to a small set of candidate patterns, which limits flexibility and performance. ZB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> only reschedules <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> to fill bubbles, leaving other stages underutilized. DynaPipe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> focuses on scheduling micro batch execution to mitigate data heterogeneity, but it lacks mechanisms to address model architectural heterogeneity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Motivation</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Increasing Heterogeneity in Model Architectures</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Nowadays, the evolution of LLMs shows a clear trend toward increasing architectural heterogeneity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lepikhin et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blakeman et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">MiniMax et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bercovich et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Instead of relying solely on standard transformer blocks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani, 2017</a>)</cite>, modern LLMs integrate diverse attention and FFN mechanisms. For example, DeepSeek <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> adopts dense FFNs in the first <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> layers (with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> manually defined) and sparse MoE layers in the later layers, and replaces SA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani, 2017</a>)</cite> with MLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. Nemotron-LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bercovich et al., 2025</a>)</cite> introduces variable FFN sizes within transformer blocks, while Minimax-M1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">MiniMax et al., 2025</a>)</cite> alternates between Lightening Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and SA. Nemotron-H <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blakeman et al., 2025</a>)</cite> combines Mamba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu and Dao, 2023</a>)</cite> and SA to form a heterogeneous attention design, and Jamba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lieber et al., 2024</a>)</cite> goes further by integrating SA, Mamba, FFN, and MoE layers into a single architecture. Beyond the hidden layers, heterogeneity also emerges in the output layer, as vocabulary sizes expand rapidly in models such as Gemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, LLaMA-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and Qwen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This growing heterogeneity in model architectures results in larger pipeline bubbles compared to previous models, such as LLaMA-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ AdaPtis: Reducing Pipeline Bubbles with Adaptive Pipeline Parallelism on Heterogeneous Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. This result highlights the necessity of adaptive pipeline parallelism to mitigate bubbles and improve training efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Demand for Adaptive Pipeline Parallelism</h3>

<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S3.T2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Taxonomy of existing pipeline parallelism approaches. <span id="S3.T2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-1.5pt;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.T2.pic3" class="ltx_picture" height="10.72" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.72 10.72" width="10.72"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.72) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.36,0) translate(0,5.36)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 M 5.36 0 C 5.36 2.96 2.96 5.36 0 5.36 C -2.96 5.36 -5.36 2.96 -5.36 0 C -5.36 -2.96 -2.96 -5.36 0 -5.36 C 2.96 -5.36 5.36 -2.96 5.36 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g></svg></span></span> : support, <span id="S3.T2.6.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-1.5pt;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.T2.pic4" class="ltx_picture" height="11.28" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 11.28 11.28" width="11.28"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,11.28) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.64,0) translate(0,5.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 M 5.36 0 C 5.36 2.96 2.96 5.36 0 5.36 C -2.96 5.36 -5.36 2.96 -5.36 0 C -5.36 -2.96 -2.96 -5.36 0 -5.36 C 2.96 -5.36 5.36 -2.96 5.36 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g></svg></span></span> : not support.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T2.7" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T2.7.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.7.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S3.T2.7.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T2.7.1.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S3.T2.7.1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> <span id="S3.T2.7.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T2.7.1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T2.7.1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T2.7.1.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Model</span></span>
<span id="S3.T2.7.1.2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
